camps in eastern Sudan are the result of  the conflicts in neighboring Ethiopia's  Northern tigray Region more than 60 000  refugees have crossed the border since  the war between Ethiopia's federal  government and the rebel tigray people's  Liberation Front or tplf started in late  2020 letter Hanan sagai her mother and  two children were among the first to  escape the conflict two years ago but  with a deal signed in November she hopes  things back home will improve so that  she and her family can return  [Music]  my husband crossed the border with us to  Sudan and then he returned back to  Humera in Tigre he said he preferred  being there even if it meant his death  he's been there since I was born in  Humira in Tigre and I was raised there I  love my country and I want to go back  and live there with my children  the deal fined between the tplf and the  Ethiopian government calls for a  ceasefire and the disarmament of the  tplf federal forces will control the  Region's airport and other major  facilities the agreement also paves the  way for Aid to reach tigray where two  years of blockade has created a  humanitarian crisis while there may now  be peace in tigray at least on paper  refugees here say returning to their  homes is not easy many lost all they had  when they fled to Sudan others say the  situation in tigray after the deal was  signed has not been encouraging  also fled to Sudan at the start of the  conflict he set up a shop here in  umrakuba Camp he and rights groups  accuse Eritrean forces that fought  alongside the Ethiopian government of  committing atrocities the deal does not  mention them but highlight says certain  conditions must be in place for him and  his family to return home  is  should leave tigray for there to be real  peace but as long as they are there  there will be no peace how can they stay  it's not their country they're killing  people they've stolen properties they've  raped women as long as they're there we  cannot go back and there won't be peace  the war has left himalayans in desperate  need of humanitarian assistance and  thousands facing famine since the deal  was signed some Aid has started  trickling in but many are still in need  refugees like gabrizgar say going back  won't be easy  if we go back to Tigre what will we eat  what will we drink what will we wear  everything has been destroyed farmlands  have been burnt even those inside have  no food if International Aid can come in  then maybe we can go back home when  there is telecommunication functioning  Banks we need all of that  the word Integra has left an impact  Beyond just the region those inside  Ethiopia hope that the coming months  bring stability but those in refugee  camps outside the country say they'll  wait to see if the deal holds and things  improve before they can return home  people Morgan Al Jazeera gadariv Eastern  Sudan
 

